---
import {
  BUILD_COSTS,
  CREDIT_USD,
  LISTED_PLAN_IDS,
  PLAN_COPY,
  PLAN_TABLE,
  PRICE_CURRENCY,
  INTERNAL_PER_CREDIT,
  TYPICAL_BUILD_CREDITS,
  buildsPerDay,
  formatCredits,
  formatMoney,
  fullRateDays,
  monthlyCeilingBitesFirst,
} from '@studpilot/shared';

// Read, never restated: this page explains the quota, so a stale copy of it here would be the
// documentation contradicting the thing it documents. Every figure is in PLAN_TABLE and BUILD_COSTS.
const free = PLAN_TABLE.free;
const paid = LISTED_PLAN_IDS.filter((id) => id !== 'free');
/* The plans whose monthly ceiling arrives before the month does: on each of them the daily figure
   alone is not what a reader will meet. */
const ceilingPlans = LISTED_PLAN_IDS.filter((id) => monthlyCeilingBitesFirst(id));
const small = BUILD_COSTS.find((b) => b.id === 'small')!;
const big = BUILD_COSTS.find((b) => b.id === 'big')!;
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DocsKit from '../../components/picks-docs/DocsKit.astro';
import { GENERATED_IMAGE_MAX_BYTES, GENERATED_PROJECT_BYTES, GENERATED_PROJECT_COUNT } from '../../../../worker/src/generated-image-limits';

---

<DocsLayout
  title="Credits & limits — StudPilot Docs"
  description={`How StudPilot's Credits quota works: ${free.creditsPerDay} per day free (up to ${free.creditsPerMonth} a month), what builds cost, how it is metered, daily reset, and exactly what happens when you run out.`}
  heading="Credits & limits"
>
  <p>
    Credits are StudPilot's unit of daily energy. Every AI request burns some; your balance refills to
    full once a day, <strong>up to a monthly ceiling</strong>. The system exists for one reason:
    real inference costs real compute, and a hard, transparent quota is what lets the free tier be
    genuinely free — no card, no trial clock, no surprise bill.
  </p>

  <h2>The numbers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Free plan:</strong> {free.creditsPerDay} Credits per day · up to {free.creditsPerMonth} a month.</li>
    {paid.map((id) => (
      <li><strong>{PLAN_COPY[id].name} plan:</strong> {PLAN_TABLE[id].creditsPerMonth} Credits a month · up to {PLAN_TABLE[id].creditsPerDay} a day. Planned: paid plans start later.</li>
    ))}
    <li><strong>One Credit</strong> is about {formatMoney(CREDIT_USD, { currency: PRICE_CURRENCY })} of AI compute, and your balance shows with two decimals.</li>
    <li><strong>A typical build:</strong> about {formatCredits(TYPICAL_BUILD_CREDITS)} Credits. A small one costs {formatCredits(small.creditsLow)} to {formatCredits(small.creditsHigh)}; a big one, which is still an estimate, {big.creditsLow} to {big.creditsHigh}.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Those are typical costs, not quotes: a build is charged for the compute it actually used, so a
    long one climbs past its figure. In practice the Free daily allowance is a solid daily session,
    about {buildsPerDay('free')} typical builds.
  </p>

  {/*
    TWO SENTENCES WERE REMOVED FROM THIS SECTION, AND BOTH WERE GUARANTEES NOBODY IMPLEMENTS.

    "If StudPilot estimates a run will be unusually expensive, it says so before starting instead of
    silently draining your day." There is no pre-run estimate anywhere a customer can see: the wire
    protocol has no cost-warning message, a run is admitted on a one-Credit spend whose only refusal
    is a zero balance, and the worker's own preferences.ts downgrades the `ask` tool permission to
    `deny` because "nothing in this product can interrupt a run to ask". The paragraph above already
    describes what the app shows truthfully and stands on its own; a warning BEFORE the spend is
    a different promise, and it was not kept.

    THIS PARAGRAPH SAID "NOTHING COMES BACK" AND THAT STOPPED BEING TRUE on 2026-09-20, when
    QuotaDO gained a /refund route and SessionDO began calling it. The sentence was correct when it
    was written and a guard in apps/site/tests/credit-refund-claims.test.mjs failed the moment the
    route appeared, which is the only reason this was not left published and wrong.

    What is true now is narrow, and the narrowness is the point: a run that ENDED IN FAILURE and
    left you nothing you can keep — no applied operations, nothing changed in your place, no
    requested image or model, and in a conversational mode no answer — gives its Credits back
    automatically, and the reply says so. A run that changed your place keeps its Credits however
    it ended, because you still have what it built. Pressing stop is not a refund. The refund is
    idempotent and is composed from what the ledger actually returned rather than from what was
    asked for, because the two differ over a UTC midnight and the difference is your money.
  */}
  {/*
    "ALWAYS VISIBLE IN THE WORKSPACE HEADER, AND IT UPDATES LIVE" WAS WRONG TWICE.

    Where: the balance meter is in the FOOTER OF THE LEFT RAIL, not a header —
    apps/web/src/components/layout.tsx renders <UsageMeter> inside `.gx-rail__foot`.

    AND THEN "IN THE FOOTER OF THE LEFT RAIL" WAS WRONG TOO, in the state a first-time reader is
    actually in. Measured on the deployed app on 2026-09-20 with a fresh browser profile at 1920,
    1440, 1280 and 1024: the shell renders `gx gx-shell is-rail-collapsed`, there is NO `.gx-usage`
    element in the document at all, and `.gx-rail__foot` does not exist either. What the collapsed
    dock holds is a row labelled "Usage and Credits". Click "Open navigation" and the rail appears
    with the meter in its footer, reading "202 Credits left today / Free / of 231 a day / about 2
    more builds / resets in 14h" — so the old sentence was true only AFTER an action this page
    never told anybody to take. A reader following it looked at a rail that was not there and
    concluded the product had lost their balance.

    So the paragraph names the dock row first, because that is what is on screen when the workspace
    loads, and the rail footer second, as what you get once the navigation is open. The Credits page
    it links to now also carries what the next request costs, per mode, beside the balance —
    apps/web/src/routes/usage.tsx, derived from MODE_INFO the same way the table above is.

    Live: it is not. The meter is fed from `me.data?.quota`, a react-query ['me'] query with
    staleTime 60s, and apps/web/src/app.tsx sets refetchOnWindowFocus: false. Only two actions in
    Settings ever invalidate it. The worker DOES push a live `quota` socket message — the hook
    handles it and exposes `quota` (apps/web/src/lib/use-project-socket.ts) — but workspace.tsx
    does not destructure `quota` from the hook, so the update is received and dropped. The figure a
    customer watches for an hour is the one from page load.

    NOR IS THE PER-RUN TOTAL LIVE. This note used to say "What IS live is the per-run total:
    thinking.tsx renders '<n> Credits this run' from status.creditsSpent while the run works".
    thinking.tsx was removed on 2026-10-01 and nothing in apps/web renders `status.creditsSpent`:
    the only cost the app draws is the settled figure under a finished reply (ws/turn.tsx). So no
    sentence here or on /pricing may say a run's cost is counted while it runs; the guard is
    apps/site/tests/no-live-cost-claim.test.mjs. Wiring the balance is an apps/web fix.
  */}
  <h2>How metering works</h2>
  <p>
    Your balance is one click away in the workspace: the dock down the left edge carries a
    <strong>Usage and Credits</strong> row, and it opens a page with the figure, the last thirty
    days, and what your next request costs. The left navigation is closed when the
    workspace loads, so the meter itself is not on screen until you open it — once you do, it sits
    in the footer of the left rail beside your account, reading what is left, what your allowance is
    and when it resets. Either surface is read when the page loads and does not refresh itself while
    you build — reload for the current figure. The app shows a request's cost under its reply once
    the reply has finished, not while it works. A request is let in for
    1/{INTERNAL_PER_CREDIT} of a Credit, and the rest settles against the compute the run actually
    consumed after each model call, so a long build can cost more than the figures above.
  </p>
  <p>
    A step that fails on our side is not charged: Credits are settled from the compute a call
    actually reported, and a call that returned nothing reports nothing. Beyond that, a run that
    ends in failure having left you nothing to keep gives its Credits back on its own — the reply
    tells you how many and you do not have to ask. A run that changed your place keeps its Credits
    whatever went wrong afterwards, because the work is still there; and stopping a run yourself is
    not a failure, so it is not refunded.
  </p>

  <h2>The daily reset — and the monthly ceiling behind it</h2>
  <p>
    The daily limit resets at midnight UTC — one fixed instant for every account, not a rolling window
    that starts when you do. That is worth knowing if you build late in the UTC day: credits
    spent at 23:00 UTC are refilled an hour later. The monthly limit resets at 00:00 UTC on the 1st of
    the next month, and a new day does not lift it. The workspace says which of the two stopped you and
    shows its reset time. Unused Credits do not roll over — every day starts at your full allowance.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>There are two limits, and you have whichever is smaller.</strong> The daily figure is a
    rate; the monthly figure is a ceiling, and a day's refill cannot take you past it.
    {ceilingPlans.length > 0 ? (
      <>
        {' '}On every plan the ceiling is the one that bites if you spend the whole daily allowance
        every day:{' '}
        {ceilingPlans.map((id, i) => (
          <>
            {i > 0 ? '; ' : ''}{PLAN_COPY[id].name} gets {PLAN_TABLE[id].creditsPerDay} a day against{' '}
            {PLAN_TABLE[id].creditsPerMonth} a month, which is <strong>{fullRateDays(id)} full days</strong>
          </>
        ))}
        . After that nothing is left until the month turns. Pace it and the same total stretches
        across the month instead.
      </>
    ) : (
      <>{' '}On every current plan the monthly ceiling clears a full month, so the daily figure is the
        one you will meet in practice.</>
    )}
    {' '}The usage meter names which of the two you are up against rather than showing one number for
    both.
  </p>

  <h2>Running out</h2>
  <p>When your balance hits zero:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>New AI requests are declined with a clear message and your reset time — never a silent failure.</li>
    <li>Everything that does not burn inference keeps working: reading chat history, browsing your project, creating and restoring checkpoints, pairing Studio.</li>
    <li>Nothing is deleted, downgraded or lost. You are just out of energy until the reset.</li>
  </ul>

  {/*
    THE SHARED CEILING WAS NOWHERE ON THIS PAGE, AND /docs/troubleshooting SENDS READERS HERE FOR IT.

    That page's capacity paragraph ends "See Credits & limits for the difference between the two" —
    and there was no second limit here to be different from. A customer met the service-wide wall
    for the first time as an error message, having read the page that was supposed to have told
    them. Described without a figure on purpose: the ceiling lives in apps/worker/src/pricing.ts and
    is the owner's to move, and a number copied onto a marketing page is a number free to drift.
    What does not drift is that the limit exists, is shared, and is not the reader's allowance.
  */}
  <h2>The limit that is not yours</h2>
  <p>
    Your allowance is not the only ceiling. StudPilot also caps what the <em>whole service</em> spends
    on inference in a day, shared by everyone using it, and that cap can be reached while your own
    balance still has Credits in it. A run stopped by it says so in its own words — “StudPilot has
    reached today's shared building capacity” — and it resets at midnight UTC like everything else
    here. It exists so that one runaway build cannot take the free tier down for the rest of the
    day. <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> covers what a run that hits it costs
    you and what it leaves behind.
  </p>

  <h2>Project files</h2>
  <p>
    StudPilot keeps notes, plans and generated data in a workspace beside each project — StudPilot's own
    storage, not your Roblox place — and you can open, add, rename and download those files from the
    Files drawer in the workspace. It holds <strong>text files only</strong>: .md, .txt, .json,
    .csv, .luau, .lua, .ts, .js, .yml and .yaml. Generated images are separate: download them
    from their result in the conversation. Roblox models live in your place.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>48 KB per file.</strong> A file past that is refused rather than truncated, so a
      half-saved document is never what you find later. Long material belongs in several files.</li>
    <li><strong>20 earlier versions of each file are kept.</strong> Every write archives the one
      before it, and any kept version can be read and put back — putting one back is itself a new
      version, so nothing in between is lost.</li>
    <li><strong>Deleted files are recoverable for 30 days.</strong> Delete moves a file to the
      project's trash with a real deadline on it, and the drawer says how long each one has left.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    None of this counts against your Credits. Credits pay for inference; files are storage, and the
    storage has the limits described here.
  </p>

  <h2>Generated images</h2>
  <p>
    New generated images stay with their project until it is deleted. Each image can be up to{' '}
    {GENERATED_IMAGE_MAX_BYTES / 1024 / 1024} MiB, with at most {GENERATED_PROJECT_COUNT} images
    and {GENERATED_PROJECT_BYTES / 1024 / 1024} MiB of encoded image data per project
    (encoding uses about a third more space than the downloaded file).
    Download a copy to keep outside StudPilot. Older temporary images that have expired cannot be recovered.
  </p>
  <p>
    Generation is refused before starting when project or service storage is full. Existing images
    are not removed to make room. Concurrent runs can still fill the space while an image is being
    generated; if saving fails, StudPilot reports that failure instead of claiming delivery.
  </p>

  <h2>Other limits worth knowing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rate limiting:</strong> requests are queued per account, and under heavy load a request may briefly wait. No plan buys a place further up that queue — paying changes your allowance, not your turn.</li>
    <li><strong>Checkpoints:</strong> StudPilot keeps your 25 most recent checkpoints per project. Taking the twenty-sixth deletes the oldest in the same write — by age, whether you took it yourself or StudPilot took it before a change. Restore the one you want before it falls off the end.</li>
    <li><strong>One account per person:</strong> multiplying free accounts to farm quota is against the <a href="/terms">terms</a> and gets caught.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Making Credits go further:</strong> give StudPilot specific requests (“the door in Workspace.Lobby”) rather than vague ones. Nothing else moves the number much —
      the expensive request is almost always the one that had to be sent twice.
    </p>
  </div>

  <DocsKit />
</DocsLayout>
